<document xmlns="urn:fontes:resource:1" id="rom-001" resource-id="org.example.romans-study">
  <title>The opening of Romans</title>
  <annotations><scripture start="Rom.1.1" end="Rom.1.7" relation="applies-to" basis="author"/></annotations>
  <block id="rom-001-heading" kind="heading"><text>Identity and ancestry</text></block>
  <block id="rom-001-p01" kind="paragraph">
    <text>This example directs readers to <ref start="Rom.1.3">Romans 1:3</ref> and demonstrates an explicit reference attached to a paragraph.</text>
  </block>
  <block id="rom-001-p02" kind="paragraph">
    <text>A study can discuss the <term concept="urn:fontes:concept:incarnation">incarnation</term> while keeping its topic annotation distinct from the verse reference itself.</text>
    <annotations><concept id="urn:fontes:concept:incarnation" relation="discusses"/></annotations>
  </block>
  <block id="rom-001-p03" kind="paragraph">
    <text>The paragraph discusses the Messiah's human ancestry. Its reference is supplied as metadata because the verse number is not written in the prose.</text>
    <annotations>
      <scripture start="Rom.1.3" relation="alludes-to" basis="author"/>
      <concept id="urn:fontes:publisher:org.example:concept:messianic-ancestry" relation="discusses"/>
    </annotations>
  </block>
  <block id="rom-001-p04" kind="paragraph">
    <text>A word-study note can point to <strongs code="G5207">a Strong's dictionary entry</strongs>. A dictionary becomes available when installed; the code can already be indexed.</text>
    <annotations><scripture start="Rom.1.3" relation="applies-to" basis="author"/><strongs code="G5207" relation="discusses"/></annotations>
  </block>
